course: dictionary
lesson: 4924
title: Impair: when something gets weaker
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impair is a formal word. It means to damage something, or to make it weaker or worse. «Loud noise can impair your hearing.» The noise harms your ears, so your hearing gets worse. That is what impair means.
explain: Look for the clue. Ask yourself: what can get weaker or worse? Hearing, breathing, thinking and eyesight can all be impaired. If the sentence is talking about something getting worse or being harmed, impair probably fits.
explain: Impaired is the adjective form, and it describes something that has been weakened. «Visually impaired» means someone whose eyesight is weakened. It is the official, respectful term, and it is kinder than a rude label.
fact: Impair means to damage something or make it weaker or worse.
fact: Impair is a formal word, so you will meet it more often in serious writing than in everyday chat.
fact: Loud noise, such as very loud music, can impair your hearing.
fact: Smoke can impair your breathing, and lack of sleep can impair your thinking.
fact: «Visually impaired» and «hard of hearing» are respectful ways to describe a person, because they describe what is happening, not a rude label.
exercise: «Loud noise can impair your hearing.» What does impair mean here? | *To damage something or make it weaker | To make something louder | To hear a sound very clearly | To fix something completely
exercise: «Lack of sleep can impair your thinking.» What might lack of sleep do to your thinking? | Make you laugh more | Make it faster and sharper | *Make it weaker or worse | Make it stronger every day
exercise: «Visually impaired» describes a person. What does it mean? | Perfectly sharp eyesight | *Eyesight that is weakened, so they see less well | Very sleepy all the time | Able to see in the dark
test:
Which word is closest in meaning to impair? | Improve | *Weaken | Celebrate | Measure
«Smoke can impair your breathing.» What does impair mean here? | To help | *To damage or make worse | To colour | To warm up
exam:
Mia says «visually impaired» is a rude label. Is she right? | Yes, it is rude and should be avoided at school | *No, it is a respectful, official term for weakened eyesight | Yes, but only when someone is very old | No, it means someone is very clever
